Given that the de-Broglie wavelength of proton (\(\lambda_p\)) and electron (\(\lambda_e\)) are the same.
\lambda_p = \lambda_e
The de-Broglie wavelength formula is \(\lambda = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2mK}}\) where \(h\) is Planck's constant, \(m\) is mass, and \(K\) is kinetic energy.
\frac{h}{\sqrt{2m_p K_p}} = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2m_e K_e}}
Squaring both sides and cancelling \(h^2\) and \(2\):
\Rightarrow \frac{1}{m_p K_p} = \frac{1}{m_e K_e}
Rearranging to find the ratio of kinetic energies \(K_p/K_e\):
\frac{K_p}{K_e} = \frac{m_e}{m_p}
Given that \(m_p = 1836 \, m_e\):
