For a non-reflecting surface, the force \(F\) exerted by light of intensity \(I\) on an area \(A\) is given by:
F = \frac{IA}{c}
Where \(c\) is the speed of light. We need to find the area \(A\), so we can rearrange the formula:
A = \frac{Fc}{I}
Given values:
• Force \(F = 2.4 \times 10^{-4}\) N
• Intensity \(I = 360\) W/cm\(^{2}\)
• Speed of light \(c = 3 \times 10^{8}\) m/s
First, convert the intensity from W/cm\(^{2}\) to W/m\(^{2}\):
I = 360 \text{ W/cm}^2 = 360 \times (100 \text{ cm/m})^2 \text{ W/m}^2 = 360 \times 10^4 \text{ W/m}^2
Now, substitute the values into the formula for \(A\):
A = \frac{(2.4 \times 10^{-4}) \times (3 \times 10^{8})}{360 \times 10^{4}}
A = \frac{2.4 \times 3 \times 10^{(-4+8)}}{360 \times 10^{4}}
A = \frac{7.2 \times 10^{4}}{360 \times 10^{4}}
A = \frac{7.2}{360}
A = 0.02 \text{ m}^2
The area of the surface is \(0.02\) m\(^{2}\).

